<DocsPage
  title="Connect CLIs & Models"
  description="Molt Code drives the coding CLIs installed on your machine. Install one, authenticate it, and it becomes available as an engine for your agents."
  currentPath="/docs/connect-clis/"
  next={next}
>
  <h2 id="how-harnesses-work">How Harnesses Work</h2>
  <p>
    Molt Code does not implement its own coding agent. It drives the CLIs you
    already use — Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode and others — as managed
    subprocesses, and gives them a workspace, context, and somewhere for their
    output to land.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each CLI is an <strong>engine</strong>. When you create an agent you pick one
    engine and one model; the agent's identity and instructions sit on top. This
    is why swapping an agent onto a newer model costs you nothing — the teammate
    you built survives the engine change.
  </p>
  <p>
    Engines currently available through the adapter registry include
    <strong>Claude Code</strong>, <strong>Codex</strong>,
    <strong>OpenCode</strong>, <strong>Cursor</strong>,
    <strong>Copilot</strong>, <strong>Antigravity</strong>, and
    <strong>Pi</strong>. The list is registry-driven rather than hardcoded, and
    availability varies by platform, so the authoritative list is the one shown
    in the app.
  </p>

  <h2 id="installing-a-cli">Installing a CLI</h2>
  <p>
    Install the CLI as you normally would, following its own documentation. Molt
    Code detects the engines present on your machine and offers them when you
    create or edit an agent.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you already run one of these from your terminal, it is already connected.
    There is no separate Molt Code plugin to install.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      Molt Code seeds a default agent for each installed engine, named after the
      engine itself. If you open the Agents screen and find <code>@claude-code</code>
      already there, that is why. Rename it, re-instruct it, and make it yours —
      nothing is re-seeded once an engine has an agent.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="authentication">Authentication</h2>
  <p>
    Each CLI handles its own authentication, and Molt Code uses whatever
    credentials that CLI already has. Log in with the CLI's own command and you
    are done.
  </p>
  <p>
    Depending on the engine that means either a provider subscription login or
    your own API key. Either way the credential belongs to the CLI on your
    machine — Molt Code does not proxy your requests, does not broker your keys,
    and takes no cut of your tokens.
  </p>
  <p>
    For secrets that your <em>projects</em> need — a database URL, a deploy
    token — see <a href="/docs/permissions/">Permissions &amp; Safety</a>. Those
    are a separate mechanism from CLI authentication.
  </p>

  <h2 id="choosing-a-model">Choosing a Model</h2>
  <p>
    Models are offered per engine; the choices come from the engine's own
    catalog, so a newly released model appears once the CLI supports it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Practical guidance: match the model to the agent's job rather than always
    reaching for the largest. A reviewer or an architect benefits from a strong
    model at high effort. An agent that renames things across forty files does
    not, and will finish considerably faster on something lighter.
  </p>
  <p>
    Effort, permission mode, and sandbox mode are set per agent alongside the
    model, and the available choices depend on the engine — they reset when you
    change it. See <a href="/docs/agents/">Create &amp; Customize Agents</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="agent-unavailable">Agent Unavailable</h2>
  <p>
    If an agent will not run, the cause is nearly always one of three things:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The CLI is not installed</strong> — or not on the <code>PATH</code> that Molt Code sees. Confirm by running it directly in a terminal.</li>
    <li><strong>The CLI is not authenticated</strong> — its login expired. Re-authenticate with the CLI's own command.</li>
    <li><strong>The model is not available to your account</strong> — pick another on the agent.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A run that fails for one of these reasons appears in the thread as a failed
    reply with the error attached, rather than silently doing nothing. See
    <a href="/docs/troubleshooting/">Troubleshooting</a> for the full list.
  </p>
</DocsPage>
